WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.440
If you remember, Nora was puzzled over how

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.530
to find the instantaneous speed of the ball

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:12.922
. She wanted to know the speed of the ball

00:00:12.922 --> 00:00:15.990
at the INSTANT it was at position ‘B’.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.828
In this video, we will see that the answer

00:00:18.828 --> 00:00:21.599
to this question gives rise to one of the

00:00:21.599 --> 00:00:26.080
central idea of calculus called differentiation.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.133
And this idea is built upon the idea of limits

00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:30.859
that we discussed earlier.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.980
Anyway, do you remember the difficulty we

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:37.600
faced in finding the instantaneous speed?

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.120
If an object is travelling with a constant

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.300
speed, we can easily determine its speed at

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:44.780
any instant of time.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.270
At every instant, the speed will be the same.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:52.155
It will be equal to the total distance travelled

00:00:52.155 --> 00:00:54.739
by the ball divided by the total time taken

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.580
to cover that distance.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.433
But we know that in reality, as we drop the

00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:04.140
ball, its speed keeps on increasing.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.718
So in our case, this formula only tells us

00:01:06.718 --> 00:01:08.560
the average speed of the ball.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.868
If we blindly apply this formula to find the

00:01:11.868 --> 00:01:14.800
instantaneous speed at position ‘B’, we

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.085
will get the answer to be zero over zero;

00:01:18.085 --> 00:01:20.409
which doesn’t make any sense.

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:23.570
So how should we approach the problem?

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:27.102
At the moment, we only know how to find the

00:01:27.102 --> 00:01:29.320
average speed of an object.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.680
So let’s play around with this concept and

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.020
see if we can find any headway.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.820
Nora had earlier figured out the average speed

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.540
of the ball after it started its motion and

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.500
reached midway at position ‘B’.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.360
It turns out to be ‘fifty centimetres per second’.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:52.280
Can you tell me what does this average speed tells us?

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.620
It means that on an AVERAGE, the speed of

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.280
the ball is ‘fifty’ as it travels this

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.210
distance.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.460
We could think of it as a ball park figure

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:06.710
that gives us an idea of the speed in this

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:08.020
duration!

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.320
Now let me ask you an easy question!

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.290
We know that the speed of the ball initially is zero.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.980
And we know that the average speed in this

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.820
time duration is 50.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.140
So what can we tell about the instantaneous

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.600
speed of the ball at position B?

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.220
Which of these options will be correct?

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:35.200
Intuitively, we can say that it’ll be greater than 50!

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.540
Why?

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.894
We know that as the ball falls, its speed

00:02:39.894 --> 00:02:42.720
keeps on increasing from zero till it reaches

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:43.980
the floor.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.703
So for some initial part, it will be less

00:02:46.703 --> 00:02:48.230
than the average speed.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.341
Hence, after some point, the ball has to speed

00:02:51.341 --> 00:02:54.250
up and travel with a speed GREATER than the

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:55.760
average speed.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.720
Hence, its speed as it reaches ‘point B’

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.680
will definitely be greater than ‘fifty’.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.920
To understand this point, you could look at

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.180
a set of horizontal bars like this with increasing

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:09.180
lengths.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:12.800
If I tell you that the average length of these

00:03:12.806 --> 00:03:15.700
bars is 50, and the length of the first bar

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.420
is 10, then you’d be sure that the length

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.180
of the last bar will definitely be more than

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.180
50.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.760
Only then will you get the average as 50.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.000
Let’s come back to our problem now!

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.000
Now let’s say we find where the ball is

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.060
half a second after we drop it.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.040
The information we have is that it covers

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:39.560
a distance of twelve and half centimetres

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.659
and reaches this position ‘C’.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.699
You would be wondering from where we get these

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:45.600
values.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.420
Don’t worry about them for now.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.616
Ideally we can experimentally determine them

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.019
but we have calculated them using some equation

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.480
And for ease of calculation, I have taken

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.110
the acceleration due to gravity to be ‘one’

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:05.349
instead of ‘nine point eight’ metres per second squared.

00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:08.008
Now let’s get back and calculate the average

00:04:08.008 --> 00:04:10.849
speed of the ball between the starting position

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:14.960
A and ‘C’ and also between positions ‘C’ and ‘B’.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.520
Here are the formulae for average speed.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:21.840
Can you calculate these values?

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.600
The values turn out to be ‘twenty five’

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.160
and ‘seventy five’ respectively.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.570
So now the picture is little clearer.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:35.390
Now the average speed between these two points is 75.

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:40.560
So what can we tell about the ball’s speed at position B?

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.699
Based on the concept we saw earlier, the ball’s

00:04:43.699 --> 00:04:46.171
speed at ‘B’ will actually be GREATER

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:47.940
than ‘seventy five’.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.140
How?

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.980
Because we know that as the ball falls, its

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.540
speed increases continuously.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.935
So the ball’s speed at ‘B’ will be greater

00:04:59.935 --> 00:05:01.710
than that at ‘C’.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.860
And therefore to cover this distance in half

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:06.660
a second, its speed will cross this average

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.370
at some point between ‘B’ and ‘C’.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.675
With the information given, we’ve concluded

00:05:12.675 --> 00:05:15.980
that the instantaneous speed of the ball at

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:19.130
B will be definitely greater than 75.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.451
We still haven’t got the answer to the instantaneous

00:05:23.451 --> 00:05:26.110
speed of the ball at position B.

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:28.380
How do we get that answer?

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:32.260
For that, let us ask ourselves a question…

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:35.570
What happens after the ball crosses position B?

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:38.417
It’s obvious that at any point below B,

00:05:38.417 --> 00:05:41.410
the ball’s speed will be greater than its

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:42.960
speed at ‘B’.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:46.750
And that tells us something even more interesting!

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:49.380
If we find the average speed between position

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.240
‘B’ and some point D below it, we will

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:54.960
know that the instantaneous speed at ‘B’

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.680
will be less than this value.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.295
So if the average speed of the ball between

00:06:00.295 --> 00:06:02.910
B and D is say one hundred and twelve point

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:06.306
five, then we can say that the instantaneous

00:06:06.306 --> 00:06:09.470
speed of the ball at B will be definitely

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.400
LESSER than this value.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:15.660
What we’re trying to do here is get a RANGE.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.300
We will continue this in the next part.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.040
Our goal is to find the instantaneous speed

00:06:26.048 --> 00:06:28.640
at B, and we know that it is greater than

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.260
75!

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:32.583
Now we’re given some more information about

00:06:32.583 --> 00:06:34.960
what happens after the ball crosses position

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:38.250
B. At time ‘t’ equals one point two five

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:41.540
seconds the ball is at the position ‘D’,

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:45.019
which is twenty eight point one two five centimetres

00:06:45.019 --> 00:06:46.290
below position ‘B’.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:51.090
Now let’s find the average speed between ‘B’ and ‘D’.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:52.080
Can you try?

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.600
It turns out to be one hundred and twelve

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:56.890
point five.

00:06:56.890 --> 00:06:59.020
And what does this tell us?

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:02.206
As we saw before, it means the instantaneous

00:07:02.206 --> 00:07:05.320
speed of the ball at ‘B’ will be definitely

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:06.400
less than this value.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.900
So we get the speed of the ball at ‘B’

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.660
to be somewhere between ‘seventy five’

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:14.080
and one hundred and twelve point five.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.736
We’re getting closer to the answer of finding

00:07:16.736 --> 00:07:19.510
the instantaneous speed of the ball at position

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:23.610
B. It will be between these two numbers.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.667
Let’s proceed further and find the average

00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:29.870
speed of the ball between point B and points

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:31.280
closer to it.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.880
We’re given some more information now.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:37.017
We see that the ball is at positions E and

00:07:37.017 --> 00:07:40.080
F at zero point nine five seconds and one

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.080
point zero five seconds respectively.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.860
Let us try calculating the average speed in

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:47.680
these two regions.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.920
The calculations give us these two values.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:54.240
Ninety seven point five and one hundred and

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:55.840
two point five!

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:59.240
The instantaneous speed at B will be between

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:00.540
these two values!

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:04.613
Now we see that we have an even better estimate

00:08:04.613 --> 00:08:06.750
of the instantaneous speed at ‘B’.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:09.910
Did you get the idea?

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:13.120
What should be our next step?

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:16.331
Yes, we should find the average speed values

00:08:16.331 --> 00:08:19.470
between point B and the positions closer to

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:22.745
B. So , as we calculate the average speed

00:08:22.745 --> 00:08:26.020
values closer and closer to position ‘B’,

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:29.280
we get closer to the actual speed of the ball at ‘B’.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.120
We see that these numbers approach the number ‘100’.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:36.720
From the top as well as from the bottom!

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.619
If we continue to calculate the average speed

00:08:39.619 --> 00:08:42.390
in even smaller intervals, we will get even

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.970
closer to the number hundred.

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:47.339
So we can conclude that the Instantaneous

00:08:47.339 --> 00:08:49.650
speed of the ball at ‘B’ will be hundred

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.470
centimetres per second.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:54.550
This is the basic idea.

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:57.273
We get closer and closer to the answer with

00:08:57.273 --> 00:08:58.160
this approach.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:00.907
We see that the average speed approaches a

00:09:00.907 --> 00:09:03.720
number, which will be the speed of the ball

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:05.380
at position ‘B’.

00:09:05.380 --> 00:09:09.450
This process is called the LIMIT PROCESS.

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:12.080
We say that in the limit this time interval

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.710
gets closer and closer to zero; the average

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:17.880
speed of the ball approaches the instantaneous

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:19.120
speed of the ball.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:22.370
Nora’s problem is now resolved.

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:25.035
She knows a way to find the instantaneous

00:09:25.035 --> 00:09:26.270
speed of an object.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:30.453
She realises that understanding Calculus is easy:

00:09:30.453 --> 00:09:33.440
it is just our knowledge of algebra

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:34.920
stretched to a limit.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:38.696
Now let me tell you that this is the basic

00:09:38.696 --> 00:09:41.290
idea on which the concept of differentiation

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:43.140
is based.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:44.670
What do we mean by it?

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:47.768
The speed of an object measures how its position

00:09:47.768 --> 00:09:49.640
changes with respect to time.

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:52.400
It is nothing but the RATE of change of the

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.520
position of the object.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:56.933
What we did here near point B, is that we

00:09:56.933 --> 00:09:59.630
looked at how the position of the ball changes

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:02.700
in very very small time intervals.

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.720
And then we found the instantaneous speed

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:05.860
of the ball at ‘B’.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:09.536
The process by which we found this rate of

00:10:09.536 --> 00:10:13.180
change at an instant is called differentiation.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.700
We will see that differentiation helps us

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:18.320
in analysing how things change.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.800
Using the concept, we can find the instantaneous

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:23.800
rate of change of any quantity.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:26.520
We will learn more about it in our upcoming videos.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.420
In the next video we will further explore

00:10:29.421 --> 00:10:32.810
this idea and see how it is related to another

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:36.220
problem we discussed earlier, which was about

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:39.740
finding the areas of shapes.

